+	a bitmask that can uniquely represent each player. Living World: widened from a 16-bit integer to a
+	fixed array of 32-bit words so the cap can exceed 16. Plain data (no virtuals) so Xfer can still copy it as bytes.
+	Integer conversions are kept for the legacy spellings (`= 0`, `== 0`, `!= 0`, BitTest/BitSet/BitClear).
